<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
    <subsection xml:id="gettingmore"><title>Getting More from Class</title>
	    <introduction>
    <p>
    Learning cannot take place solely in the classroom, and you should expect to put in considerable work outside of class. Different students learn in different ways and require different types of support. I am committed to providing you with the support that you require, whether that means being a critical questioner of your processes and results, providing guidance and mentoring, or something else entirely.
    </p>
    <p>
            This section has some advice and perspectives that I hope will help you make the most of your time and effort in this class.
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsubsection xml:id="go_to_student_hours">
            <title>Attend Student Hours</title>
    <p>
    First, never hesitate to come to me during <xref ref="contact" text="custom">student hours</xref> or to
    make an appointment to see me.  My primary job is to facilitate your
    understanding and learning, so when you come to me you are allowing me to do my
    job well.
    </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection xml:id="form_study_groups">
            <title>Form a Study Group</title>
    <p>
    You may also find that working with a group of classmates is beneficial.  I
    encourage you to form groups to work together outside of class.  Working with a
    group also allows you to see things from different perspectives. 
    </p>
    <p>
    If you or your study group have questions, please come to me and we will work through them together.
	    If my <xref ref="contact" text="custom">student hours</xref> are at times that you cannot make due to other commitments,
            <url href="mailto:wellsc@gvsu.edu" visual='mailto:wellsc@gvsu.edu' >make an appointment</url> to see me at another
    time.
    </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection xml:id="attendance">
	    <title>Make Attendance a Priority</title>
    <p>
	    As discussed in <xref ref="assessment" text='title' />, attendance in class is an
    important part of this course.  You are expected to be present and on time each day we meet.  You
    are responsible for announcements made in class concerning material covered, assignments, changes in
    the syllabus or due dates, or anything else pertinent to the course.  Furthermore, many of the
    activities from class and reflections on class will become part of your portfolio.
    </p>
    <p>
	    Nevertheless, your health and safety and that of your classmates is important.  Therefore <em>if you are ill, stay at home</em>.  In the event of a University recognized absence, arrangements can be
    made for you to mitigate the effects of missing class.
    </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection xml:id="preparation">
    <title>Come to Class Prepared</title>
    <p>
    It is imperative that you work on a consistent basis. 
    This applies to both the day-to-day work to prepare for class as well as the more long-term work such as the portfolio.   
            </p>
            <p>
    You should keep a well-organized record of your study notes, completed problems, and problems in
    progress for future reference. Note that these are acceptable as part of your portfolio if you
    believe they help make your case!
    </p>
    <p>
    You must understand that a great deal of your learning in this course must occur on your own.  
    It is your responsibility to complete assigned reading, read assignments carefully, complete the
    problems, be prepared for class, and to seek support as needed.
    </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection xml:id="participation">
    <title>Participate in Class</title>
    <p>
            For many people, their experience has taught them that asking questions or questioning results is frowned on as either disrespectful or inattentive. In this class, I want to cultivate an atmosphere where questioning processes, results, and reasoning is seen as showing respect that the other person's ideas are worth thinking about. This type of respectful questioning (including questioning your own processes, thoughts, and results) is crucial to becoming an effective and flexible thinker.
    </p>
    <p>
            Asking and answering questions are not the only ways, or even the best ways, to participate in class. While we may think of class participation in terms of speaking up in class, participation can take many forms, such as active listening in groups, writing up or journaling individual or group assignments, drawing graphs or creating visual representations, sharing strategies, questioning results, and many others. In every class meeting, there will be ample opportunity for you to actively participate.  
    </p>
    </subsubsection>
    <subsubsection xml:id="additional_resources">
            <title>Make Full Use of Additional Learning Resources</title>
            <p>
                            The idea of <q>asking for help</q> can sometimes take on a negative connotation. I prefer to think of it as one way to make use of all of your available resources. If you need to travel from the Allendale campus to the Grand Rapids, you probably don't think that taking the Laker Line instead driving a car is a bad thing, it's simply another effective way to travel. And there are also lots of reasons why some students might not have access to a car, including having a flat tire or not having a license. In a similar way, there are lots of reasons to seek out additional learning resources.
            </p>
            <p>
                    In addition to student hours and individual meetings with me, GVSU provides a variety of tutoring resources, including the 
                    <url href="https://www.gvsu.edu/tutoring/math/" visual='gvsu.edu/tutoring/math' >Math Center</url>
                    (located in Mackinac Hall). More information about other tutoring services can be found on the
                    <url href="https://www.gvsu.edu/tutoring/" visual='gvsu.edu/tutoring' >GVSU Tutoring</url>
                    website.
            </p>
            <p>
                            Using these resources is <em>smart</em>, and can be beneficial to every student.
            </p>
    </subsubsection>
    </subsection>
